@play.cashy At his peak, Danny Green was one of the most valuable role players in the NBA, even if he wasnât a star in the traditional sense. His game was built around elite â3-and-Dâ playâknocking down three-pointers at a high level while guarding the opposing teamâs best perimeter players. Offensively, he was deadly as a catch-and-shoot threat, especially in big moments. During the 2013 NBA Finals with the San Antonio Spurs, he caught fire from deep and set a Finals record for most three-pointers made at the time, showing how impactful he could be on the biggest stage.

Defensively, Green was just as important. He had great instincts, quick hands, and strong positioning, allowing him to guard multiple positions and rack up steals and blocks as a guard. He was named to the All-Defensive Second Team in 2017, which highlights how respected he was on that end. More importantly, he was a winning playerâsomeone who fit perfectly into championship systems. He won titles with the San Antonio Spurs, Toronto Raptors, and Los Angeles Lakers, proving he could contribute to elite teams in different situations.

